- claims[36]: "the 2 split rows (TC-F0-N14 C0/C2 merge, TC-F0-N15 WCC/SCC merge) need no new class and are d..." — the merge assertion pattern matched? Why? `_MERGE_ASSERT` matched something in the 60-char window. Let me look at the full statement. The context is "...need no new class..." — "no new class" → hmm, _MERGE_ASSERT matches "no new class"? Let's see: `_MERGE_ASSERT` = r"as\s+one|are\s+one|is\s+one|one\s+class|single\s+class|one\s+schema|single\s+schema|unified|unif(y|ication)|merged|merge\b|share[sd]?\s+one|combined|same\s+class|treated?\s+as\s+one|into\s+a\s+single". In claims[36], "TC-F0-N14 C0/C2 merge" — "merge" matches `merge\b` right after "C0/C2 ". So the assertion is the *case label* itself: "TC-F0-N14 C0/C2 merge". The context includes "need no new class" → but _NEG_BEFORE_ASSERT only checks text before the assert match. The match "merge" comes right after C0/C2 so before = "...TC-F0-N14 " — no negation. So it's flagged.

- claims[94/96/97]: "2 SPLIT_REQUIRED (TC-F0-N14 merged C0/C2 regularities; ..." → "merged" follows "C0/C2"? Actually pattern matched "C0/C2" then context contains "merged" → flagged. It's a case label quoted.
